17. What is chunk-oriented processing?
It is a model where items are read and processed in chunks, i.e., a group of items is read → processed → written in a transactional boundary.

18. How to achieve parallel processing in Spring Batch?

Parallel Steps using Flow
Partitioning
Multi-threaded step (taskExecutor)
Remote Chunking
Remote Partitioning

19. What is partitioning in Spring Batch?
Dividing a step into multiple slave steps that can run in parallel (e.g., different data ranges).

20. What is the difference between partitioning and chunking?

| Feature     | Partitioning          | Chunking                     |
| ----------- | --------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Granularity | Whole step            | Within a step                |
| Use case    | Parallel across nodes | Processing large data sets   |
| Scope       | Split workload        | Efficient DB/file operations |

21. What happens if one record fails during chunk processing?

It retries (if retry is enabled)
Or skips (if skip is configured)
Else, the step fails and rolls back the entire chunk

22. How to handle large files in Spring Batch efficiently?

Stream readers (StaxEventItemReader, FlatFileItemReader)
Use chunk size and paging
Avoid holding entire file in memory

23. Can we run multiple jobs in parallel?
Yes, using multiple JobLauncher threads or async TaskExecutor.

24. How do you schedule Spring Batch jobs?
Using Spring Scheduler, Quartz, or external schedulers like Control-M, Airflow, Cron, Jenkins.

